/-! # Lifting split-torus homomorphisms over complete local rings

Source: Conrad, Reductive group schemes (2014), Corollary B.3.5, printed pp.292--293,
restricted to a split torus as source.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-complete-local-split-torus-hom-lift).

The torus is the existing relative split torus. Special fibres and reduction of
homomorphisms are the actual group-object pullback along the residue-field quotient.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A prescribed residue-field homomorphism from a split torus lifts over a complete
Noetherian local ring. If that homomorphism is a closed immersion, every lift is one.

Source: Conrad, Reductive group schemes (2014), Corollary B.3.5, printed pp.292--293.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-complete-local-split-torus-hom-lift).

Proof, following the accepted five-step DAG split:
1. Identify the displayed Laurent presentation with R[Z^r]: x_i maps to e_i and
   y_i to e_{-i}, and e_m maps back to the product of the corresponding positive
   and negative powers. The Hopf operations agree on these generators, as do all
   scalar extensions (lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra). This is the
   existing relativeSplitTorus. Its finite presentation and the lifting of units
   over square-zero ideals show smoothness, also in rank zero. For n >= 1 the
   ideal m^n/m^{n+1} is square-zero since 2n >= n+1.
2. Starting with f_0, recursively apply lem-sfb-square-zero-diagonalizable-hom-lift
   over R/m^{n+1} with character group Z^r. Smoothness survives base change
   (the smooth base-change clause of lem-pc-proper-base-change), and affineness
   follows from the affine fibre-product formula. Obtain compatible u_n with
   u_1 = f_0, preserving the group laws.
3. By lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-opens-finite-presentation the affine target has
   finitely generated coordinate algebra. Apply lem-sfb-adic-diagonalizable-hom-effectivity
   to this compatible family over the complete Noetherian local ring. It gives
   a group homomorphism f whose reduction at n = 1 is exactly f_0.
4. For any lift f when f_0 is closed, write its kernel as Spec(L/I), where
   L = R[Z^r] and I is generated by f^*(b) - epsilon(b). The quotient description
   (def-weil-closed-immersion and lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra)
   makes it a closed subgroup of the torus. Reduction gives ker(f_0). The
   surjective coordinate map of the closed immersion f_0 makes its kernel the
   trivial group scheme, on every affine test scheme.
5. Lem-sfb-local-torus-subgroup-special-trivial makes ker(f) trivial over R.
   If f(t) = f(t'), their quotient belongs to that kernel, so t = t' on every
   test scheme. The affine target is separated by
   lem-weil-pbc-affine-source-morphism-separated. Thus
   lem-sfb-relative-torus-monomorphism-closed makes f a closed immersion.
   This applies to every lift and includes r = 0. -/
theorem completeLocal_splitTorus_hom_lift
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    [IsAdicComplete (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) R]
    (G : Grp (Over (Spec (.of R))))
    [Smooth G.X.hom] [IsAffineHom G.X.hom] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation G.X.hom]
    (r : ℕ)
    (f₀ : (Over.pullback
        (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (IsLocalRing.residue R)))).mapGrp.obj
          (relativeSplitTorus (Spec (.of R)) r) ⟶
      (Over.pullback
        (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (IsLocalRing.residue R)))).mapGrp.obj G) :
    (∃ f : relativeSplitTorus (Spec (.of R)) r ⟶ G,
      (Over.pullback
        (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (IsLocalRing.residue R)))).mapGrp.map f = f₀) ∧
    (IsClosedImmersion f₀.hom.hom.left →
      ∀ f : relativeSplitTorus (Spec (.of R)) r ⟶ G,
        (Over.pullback
          (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (IsLocalRing.residue R)))).mapGrp.map f = f₀ →
        IsClosedImmersion f.hom.hom.left) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
